course: dictionary
lesson: 10474
title: A rare old word in context
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Some words are rare and old, so you may never have met them. Context can help. In «When its moss dries out, a tardigrade curls into a dry little barrel called a «tun»», the words dry little barrel tell you what a tun is: a container, not a sound or a weight.
explain: Be careful with words that look or sound alike. Tun is not ton, tonne or tune. Try your meaning in the sentence instead. If you say «curls into a large barrel», the sentence still makes sense, so barrel is a good guess.
explain: The sentences about tardigrades use tun for a shape, not a drink. A tardigrade is a tiny animal. When it is dry, it curls up like a little barrel and waits for water. Context tells you the tun is a shape here, and a dictionary confirms that a tun is a large barrel or cask.
fact: A tun is a very large barrel or cask.
fact: In the past, tuns were used to hold wine, beer or water.
fact: A tun was also an old unit for measuring how much liquid fits in a barrel of this size.
fact: Tun is a rare, old word. Do not mix it up with «ton», «tonne» or «tune».
fact: «Tonnage» comes from old ship measurements, based on how many tuns a ship could carry.
fact: A dried tardigrade curls into a «tun» and nearly stops all life processes.
exercise: «Old cellars and taverns may have had huge tuns of wine.» What is a tun? | A small bird's nest | A tiny insect | A type of musical instrument | *A very large barrel or cask
exercise: Ella says a tun is a tune. Why is she wrong? | Tune is a big weight and tun is a small one | A tun is a song, and a tune is a barrel | *A tun is a barrel, and tune is a song or melody | They mean exactly the same thing
exercise: «As tuns, tardigrades have survived extreme cold.» What does extreme mean here? | Ordinary and everyday | Slow and gentle | Very pleasant | *Very great or intense
test: In history, wine was carried in tuns. What were tuns used to carry? | Letters and parcels | Sand and stones | *Wine, beer or water | Birds and fish
test: «Tonnage» comes from an old measure of how many tuns a ship could hold. What does tonnage relate to? | The colour of a ship's sails | *How much a ship can carry | The number of sailors on board | How fast a ship can sail
exam: Which word fits best? «The old cellar held a huge wooden ___ of wine.» | ton | tune | *tun | tonne
